---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import NetworkDiagram from '../components/NetworkDiagram.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/?$/, '/');
---
<Base title="Get started" section="start">
  <header class="op-hero">
    <span class="eyebrow">Guide</span>
    <h1>Get started</h1>
    <p class="tagline">Install the plugins, drop your first operator, read its status. Five minutes.</p>
  </header>

  <h2><span class="num">01</span>Install</h2>
  <ol class="recipe">
    <li>Download the package for your system from the <a href="https://github.com/InfoMusCP/EyesWebTD/releases">releases page</a>: <code>EyesWebTD-&lt;version&gt;-win64.zip</code> (Windows, 64-bit) or <code>EyesWebTD-&lt;version&gt;-macos-universal.zip</code> (macOS 11 or later, Apple silicon and Intel). Unzip it: the operators are flat in <code>Plugins/</code>, one per operator (<code>.dll</code> files on Windows, <code>.plugin</code> bundles on macOS).</li>
    <li>Copy them into a <code>Plugins</code> folder next to your <code>.toe</code> (this project only) or, for all projects, into <code>Documents\Derivative\Plugins</code> on Windows or <code>~/Library/Application Support/Derivative/TouchDesigner099/Plugins</code> on macOS (create the folder if it is missing).</li>
    <li><b>macOS only:</b> the bundles are not signed by Apple, so macOS blocks them after a download. Run once in Terminal, with the folder where you copied them:<br /><code>xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine ~/Library/Application\ Support/Derivative/TouchDesigner099/Plugins</code></li>
    <li>Restart TouchDesigner. When asked, allow the custom operators to load.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="callout">TouchDesigner 2025.32280 or later, on Windows 64-bit or macOS 11+. Updating: delete the old EyesWeb files from the Plugins folder first; an old operator in another Plugins folder wins over the new one.</div>

  <h2><span class="num">02</span>Create an operator</h2>
  <p>Open the OP Create dialog (<kbd>Tab</kbd>), go to <b>CHOP → Custom</b>: every operator is listed as <code>EW L1 …</code>, <code>EW L2 …</code>, <code>EW L4 …</code> or <code>EW AP …</code>. The prefix is its <a href={`${base}concepts#layers`}>layer</a>.</p>
  <p>The palette builder (<code>palette/build_palette.py</code>) can also create a container with one instance of each installed operator and a few ready-made presets.</p>

  <h2><span class="num">03</span>Your first network</h2>
  <p>Kinetic energy from a pose: the quickest way to see the body's activity as a single number.</p>
  <NetworkDiagram chains={[['MediaPipe COMP — pose', 'Select CHOP — 33 × xyz', 'EW Kinetic Energy', 'Lag CHOP — 0.2 s', 'Null CHOP — energy'], ['EW Kinetic Energy', 'Info CHOP — status']]} caption="MediaPipe → Kinetic Energy → smoothed output, with an Info CHOP for the status" />
  <ol class="recipe">
    <li>Get a pose: the MediaPipe TouchDesigner plugin, a Kinect CHOP, OSC from a mocap system — anything that gives x, y, z per joint.</li>
    <li>Select the joints with a Select CHOP so the channels are <b>joint-major</b>: <code>j0x j0y j0z j1x j1y j1z …</code> (see <a href={`${base}concepts#layout`}>channel layout</a>).</li>
    <li>Add <b>EW L2 Kinetic Energy</b> and wire the Select into it. Leave <code>Source</code> on <em>Stream</em>.</li>
    <li>Move: <code>kinetic_energy</code> rises. Smooth it with a Lag CHOP and map it to anything.</li>
    <li>Attach an <b>Info CHOP</b> to the operator: <code>status</code> 0 is OK. Any other value comes with a reason in the Info DAT.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2><span class="num">04</span>Read the status</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:60px" class="mono">0</td><td style="width:170px"><span class="status OK">OK</span></td><td>The cook produced output.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">1</td><td><span class="status WARMING_UP">WARMING_UP</span></td><td>A window or a filter is still filling: wait, or give it more samples.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">2</td><td><span class="status NO_OUTPUT">NO_OUTPUT</span></td><td>The operator emits only on some cooks (between two hops, before the first complete window).</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="mono">3</td><td><span class="status INVALID_INPUT">INVALID_INPUT</span></td><td>The input is not usable: wrong channel count, too short, NaN, not a timeslice. The reason says why; the outputs are zero.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="muted" style="margin-top:1rem">The same names and numbers as <code>pyeyesweb.Status</code>, so a CHOP and a Python script can be compared.</p>

  <h2><span class="num">05</span>Where to go next</h2>
  <div class="grid">
    <a class="card L2" href={`${base}operators/kinematics`}><div class="top"><span>L2</span></div><h3>Kinematics</h3><p>Velocity, acceleration, jerk and curvature: the input of many other operators.</p></a>
    <a class="card AP" href={`${base}concepts`}><div class="top"><span>Guide</span></div><h3>Concepts</h3><p>Windows, streams, clips, layouts: how every operator reads its input.</p></a>
    <a class="card L1" href={base}><div class="top"><span>Catalog</span></div><h3>All operators</h3><p>Filter by layer, by analysis, or by name.</p></a>
  </div>
</Base>
